1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,226
What does it take to build a bank's e-commerce acquiring system from scratch?

2
00:00:04,226 --> 00:00:10,059
In this series we build one, part by part, and this map is where we track every step.

3
00:00:10,059 --> 00:00:18,666
On top are the three users: the merchant's server, the buyer, and the bank admin. Inside is one modular monolith with five modules.

4
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:23,416
Partner owns the merchants: who they are, and whether they may sell.

5
00:00:23,416 --> 00:00:26,464
Commission decides the fee on every payment.

6
00:00:26,464 --> 00:00:32,892
Terminal knows which bank terminal, the MID and TID, each payment goes through.

7
00:00:32,892 --> 00:00:37,107
The card vault keeps card numbers encrypted, and hands out tokens instead.

8
00:00:37,107 --> 00:00:41,630
Payment creates every payment, debits the card, and resolves the outcome.

9
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:46,904
At the edges, two ports talk to the outside world: email, and the processing center.

10
00:00:46,904 --> 00:00:56,285
Below sits the shared infrastructure: Postgres, Redis, Keycloak, Vault, Temporal, Kafka, and monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana.

11
00:00:56,285 --> 00:01:03,916
Part zero brings up Postgres, Redis, and Keycloak with a single command, and the rest arrives when a problem needs it.

12
00:01:03,916 --> 00:01:11,309
Part one builds the merchants: sign-up, email codes, two-admin approval, and API credentials kept in Vault.

13
00:01:11,309 --> 00:01:16,714
Part two adds the money rules: the commission, and the terminals we debit through.

14
00:01:16,714 --> 00:01:23,071
Part three handles cards: the Uzcard and Humo adapters, and a vault that stores only tokens.

15
00:01:23,071 --> 00:01:29,678
Part four builds the payment last, with monitoring, retries, and Kafka events, because it needs everything else.

16
00:01:29,678 --> 00:01:34,714
Part five moves the card vault into its own service, to show why clean boundaries matter.

17
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:39,190
Next, we set up the project and bring the whole world up with one command.

